"Stacks additive time series into bands to show how total magnitude and composition change together.\n\nUse when: Nonnegative components share a common timeline and sum to a meaningful total. Readers need the total trajectory alongside broad component changes.\n\nAvoid when: Precise comparison of interior components is more important than the total.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes time. Band thickness encodes a component value; the top envelope encodes their sum.\n\nBuild: Align component series on an identical time grid. Select a stable component order and calculate cumulative lower and upper boundaries. Fill bands with consistent colors, label them where space permits, and identify missing intervals.\n\nCheck: Check component sums equal the top boundary at every timestamp. Verify missing values are not automatically treated as zero. Use a zero baseline and consistent measurement units.\n\nPitfalls: Only the bottom band has a stable baseline, so apparent slopes of upper boundaries can mislead. Use separate line panels when individual component trends are the key question.\n\nExample: Show synthetic electricity generation over a week stacked by source, with total output visible as the top boundary.\n\nAlternatives: Stacked bar chart, Streamgraph, Line chart."
